<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Palatinate-Simmern</h1><p class="st-meta">1410 CE – 1598 CE · capital Simmern</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Wittelsbach line in the Hunsrück that produced the Calvinist Electors Palatine, beginning with Frederick III in 1559.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The 1410 division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupert-of-germany">King Rupert</a>'s lands gave his son Stephen the territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a>, to which his marriage added part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-sponheim">County of Sponheim</a>. In 1453 Stephen's lands were split: Frederick I received Simmern, while Louis took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palatinate-zweibrucken">Palatinate-Zweibrücken</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> dukes ruled a hilly territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> around Simmern together with their share of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-sponheim">Sponheim</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-count-palatine-of-simmern">John II</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. In 1559, when the senior electoral line died out, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-palatine">Frederick III</a> of Simmern became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> and made the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> Calvinist, issuing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> in 1563. Simmern itself then passed to a younger branch.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-palatine">Frederick III</a>'s descendants, including Frederick V, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Winter King</a>, ruled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate</a> until 1685. The junior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> branch ended with Duke Richard's death in 1598, and the duchy returned to the electorate, though a later Simmern line was briefly revived in the 17th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/palatinate-simmern.jpg" alt="Palatinate-Simmern" width="320" height="334"><figcaption>Image: JMvanDijk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Palatinate_(Palatinate-Bavaria)-Simmern.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1410 CE – 1598 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Simmern</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stephen, John II, Frederick III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pfalz-Simmern, Palatinate-Simmern-Sponheim</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1504&amp;focus=palatinate-simmern" aria-label="Palatinate-Simmern on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4855 427 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Palatinate-Simmern lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5175 683L5200 712"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5200 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5175 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5175 683h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Simmern. <a href="/atlas?year=1504&amp;focus=palatinate-simmern">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
